const body = `<h1>12.1e — Grid/Symmetry Jam: Showcase</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    The third arcade row of the year, and the last one with new
    games for a while. Grid games are the most SPECTATOR-friendly
    genre: passersby understand them in one glance, which makes
    today's showcase the most watchable yet. Star votes, retro,
    and then the road ahead.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Showcase</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Arcade row (<a href="10.1f.html">10.1f</a>
    contract): host and player in equal measure, pitch sticky by
    every keyboard.</strong> Star categories, grid edition:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>BEST BOARD:</strong> which game was
            understandable in one glance — the purest grid
            virtue</li>
        <li><strong>BEST SYMMETRY:</strong> where did the mirroring
            create fairness, difficulty, or beauty instead of
            just... mirroring</li>
        <li><strong>BEST RULE:</strong> which single rule did the
            most work per line of code</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Count the grid-game-only data: which game had the most
    passersby STOP (the one-glance test, live)?</p>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Retro</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>The four quadrants (<a href="10.1f.html">10.1f</a>
    format), plus the grid-jam question:</strong></p>
    <ol>
        <li>Went well / went wrong — one process fact each, with
            evidence</li>
        <li><strong>The grid discovery:</strong> what did the
            GRID give you that free movement would not have?
            (Every constraint jam had one — this is the
            constraint's receipt.)</li>
        <li><strong>The year-in-games question:</strong> compare
            your three jams (2-button, cards, grid). Which jam's
            CONSTRAINT taught you the most — and why does the
            answer usually rhyme with "the one that made me
            finish" (<a href="9.3a.html">9.3a</a> tradeoffs,
            artistic edition)?</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Archive + look ahead</h3>
        <ol>
            <li><code>git tag jam-grid &amp;&amp; git push --tags</code>;
                RETRO.md update with star counts and the
                grid-discovery sentence.</li>
            <li>Read the room's next dates on the
                <a href="../index.html">dashboard</a>: two
                placeholder flex days
                (<a href="12.2a.html">12.2a</a>, <a href="12.2b.html">12.2b</a>)
                close the year — topic TBA until then. Bring your
                grid jam's best idea; the placeholder jam may
                want it.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Reflect: three constraints, one lesson</h3>
        <p>
            Of the three jams you shipped this year, which constraint
            taught you the most about finishing — and what would you
            tell a team choosing their own constraint for a fourth
            jam?
        </p>
    </div>
    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Host + play with star votes and stopper-counts</li>
            <li>Name your grid discovery in one sentence</li>
            <li>Rank your three jams by what their constraints taught</li>
            <li>Leave a tagged repo and an honest RETRO.md</li>
